WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.950
YUFENG GUO: Hi, my
name is Yufeng Guo,

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:03.540
and today we're
going to look at how

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:07.470
TensorFlow Enterprise, or TFE,
builds on TensorFlow to enable

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.660
the seamless creation of
production machine learning

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.090
applications with
enterprise-grade support,

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.550
through the power
of Google Cloud.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.260
To learn about the
benefits of TFE,

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.320
let's imagine a scenario where
a data scientist visits an Ops

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.750
team to ask for help
productionizing their machine

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.135
learning platform.

00:00:23.135 --> 00:00:24.760
The question then
becomes whether there

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.620
is a suitable platform for both
experimentation and production.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:32.189
TensorFlow Enterprise is ideal
for this kind of situation.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:34.260
Google Cloud has your
solution to productionize

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.690
your TensorFlow applications.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:40.110
As part of Google Cloud, TFE
provides managed services,

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.770
cloud scale performance, and
enterprise-grade support.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.900
Let's start with
managed services

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.900
and check back in to see how
our data science and Ops teams

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:50.220
are doing.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.600
The data science team needs
development environments

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.150
with lots of compute power.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.580
And in an on-prem,
non-managed system,

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:01.290
the burden of setting them up
often falls to the Ops team.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.690
There are many challenges
to operating this way.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.480
Machines with GPUs are
expensive and take a long time

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:07.560
to acquire.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.180
Software installation
and management

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.040
consumes significant time.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.220
Additionally, there's the
problem of over-provisioning.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.260
This kind of
non-managed ML platform

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.110
doesn't scale for
enterprise-production projects.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:20.610
One solution to
these constraints

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.380
is to introduce
a standardized ML

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.930
platform for the data
scientists and Ops teams

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.760
to solve these problems.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:31.210
Enter TensorFlow Enterprise on
the Deep Learning VM, or DLVM.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:34.470
The VM is a standardized VM
image for machine learning

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:35.820
applications.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:39.960
TFE currently supports
TensorFlow 1.15 and two.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.390
The machine comes pre-installed
with the everyday tools

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.750
for data scientists, including
CUDA drivers, NumPy, Sklearn,

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.910
Pandas, and of course, Jupyter.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:50.220
Since it's a standardized
software stack,

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.560
it's easily manageable
by Ops engineers.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.230
The DLVM is deployable
to Google Cloud,

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.960
with Google Compute Engine
or AI Platform Notebooks

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.670
in just a few minutes.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.770
Also, there's no
over-provisioning problem,

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.670
because you will only
pay for the resources

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:06.370
you actually use,
minute by minute.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:08.639
Let's take a look at how you
can actually create a GCE

00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:11.340
instance with the DLVM image.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.160
In the GCP console, head on
over to the Compute Engines,

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.898
VM Instances page,
and click Create.

00:02:16.898 --> 00:02:18.690
Select Marketplace from
the left-hand menu,

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:21.490
and type Deep Learning
VM in the search bar.

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:24.060
Select the Deep Learning
VM and click Launch.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.020
In this menu, you can
select your machine type

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:27.210
and if you want GPUs.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.310
You can also select the
TensorFlow Enterprise,

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.220
versions 1.15 and two.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:35.250
For now, let's choose
TensorFlow 1.15, without GPUs,

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:40.580
and just one CPU, a very simple
image, and just click Deploy.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:43.440
The manageability you just
saw increases the portability

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.860
and scalability that you just
can't get with local notebooks.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:51.180
There's often a huge gap between
proof of concept notebooks

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.970
and production
machine learning code.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:56.070
For example, notebooks
are often particular

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:58.500
to the local environment
of each data scientist.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.740
But production code should
be portable and shareable.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.200
A single notebook instance
can't serve 10 million users.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.200
The Ops teams have to
take on a lot of work

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.620
to scale and load balance to
build a production system.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.540
Proof of concepts are
manually deployed,

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.190
whereas in production,
the Ops team

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:17.820
needs to manage a reproducible
end-to-end lifecycle.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:19.890
And after all of
that, continuous

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.540
monitoring and
alerting still need

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.070
to be taken into consideration.

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:27.330
One way to fill the gap between
experimentation and production

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:30.150
is Deep Learning
Containers, or DLC,

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:31.920
with TensorFlow Enterprise.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.220
DLC provides the exact same
standardized ML software

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:40.740
stack as a DLVM, with support
for TensorFlow 1.15 and two,

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.080
as well as lots of common
data size libraries.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.650
But now it's on a portable
and scalable container image.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:49.470
If your data science team
is already using DLVMs,

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:51.540
then it's much easier
for Ops engineers

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.270
to migrate their code
to DLC, and deploy it

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:57.690
to Google Cloud Services, such
as AI Platform and Kubernetes

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:58.680
Engine.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.840
With Deep Learning
Containers the Ops team

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.210
won't have to add
100,000 lines of code

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.050
to make a robust system.

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:08.070
For example, if you use AI
Platform or a Kubernetes engine

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:10.320
to run the container,
you automatically

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.480
get the benefits from
the managed service,

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.840
such as container scheduling and
lifecycle management, scaling

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.300
out to load balancing
and failover, logging

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.860
and monitoring, troubleshooting,
security, and more.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:24.890
Now let's see how scale Cloud
Performance of TensorFlow

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:27.080
Enterprise can benefit you.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.940
In many cases, data
scientists tend

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:31.220
to use smaller sample
data sets for building

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:33.320
their machine learning
models, and don't bother

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:35.420
handling the raw big data.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:38.870
So it falls to the Ops team
to design an ML pipeline that

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:41.330
can accommodate the 10
million rows from storage

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:43.700
without any I/O bottleneck,
and continuously

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.950
and smoothly pre-process and
train the model every day.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.940
Because TensorFlow Enterprise
provides TensorFlow runtimes

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:52.340
specifically designed
for Google Cloud,

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:55.610
you can fully utilize the cloud
scale performance of both Cloud

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:58.460
Storage and BigQuery
with minimal changes

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:00.740
to your existing
TensorFlow code.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.740
Lastly, let's talk about
enterprise-grade support.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:06.890
The pace of AI and software
versions is evolving rapidly,

00:05:06.890 --> 00:05:09.440
and it's hard to keep up with
new library versions always

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:10.640
coming out.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:12.440
That is why
TensorFlow Enterprise

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:14.720
includes long-term support.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:18.050
For TensorFlow 1.15
and two, Google Cloud

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:20.720
will provide security
patches and select bug fixes

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.550
for up to three years.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:24.920
These versions will be
available on Google Cloud,

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:27.110
and all patches and
bug fixes will also

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:30.170
be available in the mainline
TensorFlow repository.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:32.270
TensorFlow Enterprise
is available today

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:35.930
with support for
TensorFlow 1.15 and two.

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:37.400
Speed up your
software development,

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:39.590
and ensure the longevity
and reliability

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:41.750
of your AI applications
and services

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:43.710
with enterprise-grade support.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.650
It's the only offering
from the same team

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:47.120
that created TensorFlow.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.660
Head over to
g.co/cloud/TensorFlow to get

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:51.770
started today.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:57.820
[MUSIC PLAYING]

